---
// ABOUT THIS SITE — what the site is, who built it, and the way to the product
// and the company. The argument for why context matters lives on the product
// site and the Metaphori blog (owner 2026-09-14: this site is the evidence,
// the essay is one click away); what stays here is disclosure.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadRuns, loadManifest, latestEpoch, epochName } from '../lib/data'
const epoch = latestEpoch()
const manifest = loadManifest(epoch)
const runs = loadRuns(epoch)
const valid = runs.filter(r => r.validity_ok && r.score != null)
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const arms = [...new Set(runs.map(r => r.arm))].length
const models = [...new Set(runs.map(r => r.model))].length
---
<Base title="About this site" description="What the TrueArchitect benchmark site is, who built it, and where the product and the company live.">
  <div class="prose about">
    <div class="kicker">About this site</div>
    <h1>The evidence, not the argument</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This site is the public proof package behind TrueArchitect's benchmark numbers. Every page except this one is
      generated from a single export of the benchmark's own store: {runs.length.toLocaleString()} runs across
      {arms} arms and {models} models in {epochName(manifest.epoch.id)}, {valid.length.toLocaleString()} of them valid and scored,
      with every question, answer sheet, judged verdict and transcript published beside the figures. Every number on the
      site links to the run that produced it.
    </p>

    <h2>Who ran it</h2>
    <p>
      This is TrueArchitect's own benchmark. It was designed, run, scored and published by Jasdeep Jaitla, the founder of
      Metaphori, Inc., the company that builds TrueArchitect. We built the tool under test, we wrote the questions, and we
      ran every arm, including the comparison group. That is a conflict of interest, and the answer to it is the package
      itself: nothing on this site is a summary of something you cannot open. The <a href={`${base}/procedure/`}>Procedure</a>
      page states what was measured and how, the <a href={`${base}/limitations/`}>Limitations</a> page collects every
      caveat, and the <a href={`${base}/data/`}>Data</a> page points at the files and the export that made them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The argument for <em>why</em> context is the thing to measure, and the story of how this benchmark came to exist,
      are not on this site. They belong to the product and the company, and they live there.
    </p>

    <div class="brand-cards">
      <a class="brand-card" href="https://truearchitect.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="bc-word anta">TrueArchitect</span>
        <span class="bc-kicker anta">Codebase architectural intelligence</span>
        <span class="bc-body">
          The product. A desktop coding client that builds a codebase index designed for LLM consumption, so the model asks
          the index instead of reading files. The TrueArchitect arm in these results is that index behind a coding agent, at
          the same models the comparison group ran.
        </span>
        <span class="bc-link">truearchitect.ai ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="brand-card" href="https://metaphori.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="bc-logo">
          <img class="mlogo mlogo-light" src={`${base}/metaphori-logo-light.png`} alt="Metaphori logo" width="28" height="28" />
          <img class="mlogo mlogo-dark" src={`${base}/metaphori-logo-dark.png`} alt="Metaphori logo" width="28" height="28" />
          <span class="bc-word mword">Metaphori</span>
        </span>
        <span class="bc-kicker">A different approach to interpretability, from the outside in.</span>
        <span class="bc-body">
          The company. Metaphori, Inc. is an AI research company working on how semantic structure shapes attention in
          transformer models, and the maker of TrueArchitect, TrueMemory, ContextMax and ClaudeFu. The longer essay on
          context, and on why this benchmark exists, is on the Metaphori blog.
        </span>
        <span class="bc-link">metaphori.ai ↗ &nbsp;·&nbsp; metaphori.ai/blog ↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <h2>What this site records about you</h2>
    <p>
      A cookieless counter. The site loads a small script from <code>insight.truearchitect.ai</code>, a server we run, which
      records page views, the referrer, and a handful of named events: an early-access link clicked, a run directory
      opened, a figure's data downloaded, a figure control changed, a card's details opened. For a sample of visits the
      same server also records where on the page clicks land and how far the page is scrolled, kept only as an
      aggregate heat map over the page; no visit is played back. It sets no cookie, stores no IP address, and carries
      no identifier that survives the day. There is no advertising or third-party analytics on this site. The early-access links lead to
      truearchitect.ai, which is where any form lives; this site collects nothing itself. Local and preview builds of the
      site load no counter at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Reproducing it</h2>
    <p>
      The whole tree, this site included, is one repository:
      <a href="https://github.com/TrueArchitect-ai/TrueArchitect-Benchmarks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/TrueArchitect-ai/TrueArchitect-Benchmarks</a>.
      The site is a static build over <code>epochs/{manifest.epoch.id}/summary/runs.json</code>; the figures, the scorecard and
      the cards are all functions of that one file, so they cannot disagree with each other or with the runs. Re-exporting the
      epoch from the benchmark's store reproduces the tree byte for byte.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
